Stanford University so about 25 years  ago I get seriously interested in the  question of why did these new graduate  students coming into my physics research  lab how could they have done so well for  so many years in physics versus and not  be able to do physics and that led me to  first learn about and then participate  in really a new way of thinking about  science teaching and learning and this  is really the idea of approaching it  like a science and so that we we do  controlled experiments looking at  different ways of teaching and measuring  the learning that's produced between  them and figuring out then what works  and why based on data where we're  judging what works on how much students  are actually learning now this is a area  of research primarily carried out in  undergraduate science courses it was  started in physics 30 or 40 years ago  and has now spread across all the  sciences and we see quite similar  results across them and one of the  things we found is the most revealing  measures of learning are to look at  situations where a simple relevant  context as a topic you're trying to  teach and have a look at what scientists  can do when posed with this question  what predictions they make and why and  then you ask students to do the same  thing and you compare the responses  clearly you often see big differences  but this is really the measure of but I  don't argue is sort of flexible useful  knowledge how well you can apply it in  in in relevant situations and we get  very different results when we compare  that measures with what people see from  the U  of course exams which appear to be  primarily testing if students have  memorized certain facts and procedures  that are aren't good for too much beyond  passing their physics course  so using these managers of learning we  then go out and and like i say do  compared experiments and since most  classes are being taught with lectures  we compare those where the students are  spending their time listening the  instructors are mostly telling them  things with various kinds of what i'd  call sort of scientific teaching where  as we've evolved over time different  ways the instructor the students are are  practicing and and doing activities and  this and the instructors are coaching  and we're not just comparing with  lectures like this we're comparing with  ones with very charismatic engaging  lectures surprisingly it does not matter  very much and the results and there's  many many results now published on this  and i'm going to give you a few examples  so in this this is one study i was  involved with and if it was looking at  directly the amount of learning really  produced in the classroom and so we had  two big sections of introductory physics  where we carefully measured they were  very nearly identical in all respects  they were cut the two instructors were  covering the same set of learning  objectives and exactly the same amount  of class time a week of lectures and  then right at the end of that both were  given sections were given this the same  test that covered those objectives and  the comparison here was the the first  class was taught by someone was a very  experienced teacher of this material had  good student evaluations ratings and so  by the current university standards of  this and any other university would be  considered a good teacher and the other  section was was taught by someone who  was a fresh PhD in physics but had been  trained  the program I ran and these methods and  practices of scientific teaching and so  how did they do and the on the common  test at the end well you can see here  this is the this is plotting the number  of students versus their test score  random guessing would give you three and  you can see a profound difference in the  distribution between these two classes  really the the entire distribution in  the class has moved up and with of  course the experience traditional  lecture being much far below here's a  now that was just learning in the  classroom here's an example which is  looking at an entire course and also not  comparing two different instructors but  comparing the same instructors just  they're using different teaching methods  and so this has work done at Cal Poly  they looked at the concepts of force and  motion covered in this in this course  and how well students could apply these  two simple situations like a physicist  would they there's a good test for this  you use pre and post measures of how  much learning gain the students get from  class they collected data for many years  as that as these instructors were using  traditional instruction and they were  getting gains of about point three  that's we have lots of data from physics  that's pretty good for a good lecture  instruction and then they all the  instructors switched over to this  scientific teaching approach and  basically everyone jumped way up you can  see up here they're now fairly  consistent with much better learning  about a factor of two on average now the  thing I want to emphasize these are the  same people the same instructors the  only thing they changed where the  teaching methods they used and you get  greatly improved learning as a result  and you can just how great this is I  mean most I'm are bad a factor to this  person their students are learning six  times more than they were before they  chance to these scientific  methods finally this is this is not  looking at learning this is looking at  failure rates this has work done by in  computer science by best Simon who  worked with me for a while went back to  UCSD and got the instructors there to  change their teaching approaches for  these core computer science courses and  as a result after they changed over the  failure rates dropped dramatically  across all of these courses and so again  we have the same instructors just using  new teaching methods and getting much  greater student success about 1/3 the  failure rates they had before so you  probably were asked wondering at this  point you know what's actually happening  in these classes and I know I don't have  time to go into detail but basically  instead of sitting listening students  are doing tasks and so for example a  physics class on electricity would start  out with this question for all the  students this circuit with batteries and  light bulbs here and closing this switch  what's happened to the brightness of  that bulb the students are given the  task of answering individually and then  they have to discuss with their  neighbors in the class and come up with  the reason the answer and the reasons  behind it and meanwhile the instructor  like this one is circulating around  they're listening in on those  conversations maybe briefly intervening  and most importantly they're then at the  end of this question leading a follow-up  discussion where they explored the  students different reasoning processes  and give them guidance as to how to go  forward and switching topics slightly  one of the areas I've been working in is  is how we can use technology to support  this kind of of learning by students and  so in this particular class we wouldn't  just give them the answer they'd have to  build this circuit themselves using this  interactive simulations and they would  hook this up they see but the electron  cartoon electrons are doing they close  this  which and they see what happens to the  electrons and the lightbulbs and this  really allows them to do further on  their own exploration and testing and so  on and and this is a project developing  these simulations something I started a  number of years ago and it's I guess  they're gonna be used about a hundred  million times this year so it's getting  to be a fairly big deal that one of the  things I'm really interested in it as a  researcher is that these simulations let  us explore a different area of teaching  and learning than what people been able  to look at with other educational  approaches namely we can have how people  go and discover things how they figure  out things they don't know which they  can do in a simulation and it's very  hard to test that otherwise and so  really looking in their strategies for  discovery and so and our goal is really  to have people develop better  investigation processes just to give you  an idea of how one can use these kind of  things in a real course this is just an  example of the class I'm teaching now a  sophomore level quantum physics course  and they're the students I give them  this interactive simulation of a laser  and how a laser works is a very complex  process a lot of quantum physics in it  and these students are told of figured  out and they can't do it at first but  after about 20 minutes working together  in glass with a little coaching  they are successfully they're successful  in really understanding and quite a lot  of detail how a laser works and this is  only made possible by this technology  usually students really can't learn  ideas of lasers until they get much more  advanced in their academic career and of  course everybody should know about how  lasers work so if I sort of step back  all of this reasons combined it's sort  of demonstrating a fairly fundamental  principle which is that the effective  learning of science it really requires  the per  to be practicing that desired thinking  processes and getting guiding feedback  on them as they do that now and that  means when the key elements is having  carefully designed tasks that get them  to practice that you can use in  classrooms and a lot of what the ongoing  research in this field now has moved to  is really thinking about what is the  best design tasks for particular  subjects in particular areas and what's  the most effective feedback that you can  give that to have students learn most  effectively and efficiently and how does  this depend on students students  individual characteristics and then as I  mentioned we're also looking at ways we  can have technology to support this  particular learning process now a lot of  people running well how broadly does  this apply I think I can say with quite  a bit of confidence and that I'm pretty  certain that a the same basic approach  applies to other subjects and other  levels we just don't have data where  people have actually done the  comparative experiments they are to say  we do have some data from k12 it turns  out there's a there's a number of  complications in both the implementation  and actually carrying out the research  on the effectiveness there so so we  don't have as nearly is not clean and  nice of data as we have from  undergraduate classrooms but what we  have since yeah the basic ideas will  still work the same so from one  perspective you know the idea this is a  better weighted more effective way to  teach is people it's really obvious you  know if you want somebody to learn how  to play basketball you don't have them  sit every day in front of a blackboard  and be told how to play basketball you  have a break it down into particular  skills and practice those skills and get  feedback on them and from another  perspective it's it's completely  consistent and supported by a lot of  cognitive science research about how  people learn to think more like you know  experts and  science scientific reasoning and it  connects up well with the kind of work  that Bruce was talking about and the  kind of work that that Carol talks about  when you get into the details what the  people have to do to learn all of these  things show up in a very consistent  fashion but from another perspective  this is a shocking and unbelievable  result and that is you know universities  like this one and everywhere else are  filled with you know faculty giving  lectures on students about telling them  about science and they're really sure  the students must be learning from these  and they can't imagine any other way to  teach it and if we didn't have faculty  like this we wouldn't have been able to  do that experiment like I showed you at  the beginning where wait so so anyway  and you know in summary this kind of  scientific approach of carefully  measuring things and learning and  teaching it's giving dramatic results  and it's giving us important new  insights and ways we can dramatically  improve learning but you know at the  same time it's a little slow to move  from the the research into the college  classroom and to finish with a medical  analogy you know it's what we really  have is that a lot of science faculty  are really using the pedagogical  equivalent to bloodletting when they've  got you know well tested penicillin  sitting on the shelf so we we have a  ways to go but we will get there you  know you you just kind of have to  remember that if you're trying to change  a thousand-year-old tradition it does  take time even if you have science on  your side thank you  for more please visit us at stanford.edu
 

